and never include unrelated work.\n- If the checkout has overlapping user changes, is based on an ambiguous branch, or does not match the issue's repository, stop and ask the master before modifying it.\n- Do not fetch, switch branches, rewrite history, discard changes, or publish anything merely to resolve ambiguity.\n\n## 3. Clarify only when needed\n\nAfter reading the entire issue and inspecting the relevant code, identify missing decisions that would materially change the implementation, acceptance criteria, security, data handling, compatibility, deployment behavior, or public API.\n\n- Ask the master concise, specific questions for blocking ambiguities.\n- Explain the competing interpretations and their impact.\n- Pause until answered when a reasonable assumption could produce the wrong product behavior or an unsafe change.\n- If no blocking ambiguity remains, state any low-risk assumptions and continue without asking ceremonial questions.\n- Treat answers from the master as additions to the issue requirements. Re-read the issue if the master says it was updated.\n\n## 4. Implement and verify locally\n\n- Implement only the issue scope, including required tests, documentation, migrations, or generated artifacts.\n- Follow repository-native patterns and use the narrowest non-regressive change.\n- Preserve unrelated staged, unstaged, and untracked files.\n- Validate in proportion to risk. Run focused tests first, then the broader required gates.\n- Diagnose failures and distinguish issue-caused failures from pre-existing or environmental failures.\n- Do not claim completion while acceptance criteria remain unverified.\n- Do not create a branch, commit, push, pull request, release, or deployment during this stage unless the master separately expands the scope.\n\n## Linear comment hygiene\n\nBefore creating or updating a Linear comment, re-read the issue's existing comments and locate the latest comment created by this agent for the current work cycle.\n\n- Maintain one canonical completion and publication comment.\n- Retain the canonical comment ID so the same comment can be updated after publication.\n- Treat a proposed comment as a near duplicate when it repeats the same implementation summary, design decisions, verification results, and risks while only adding branch, commit, push, or pull-request metadata.\n- Update the canonical comment instead of creating a near duplicate.\n- Skip the write entirely when it adds no new information.\n- Add a follow-up comment only for a material delta, such as changed requirements, additional fixes, new verification results, a blocker or its resolution, or a new rollout or review decision.\n- Keep follow-up comments delta-only. Do not repeat unchanged implementation and verification summaries.\n- If comment updates are unavailable, post a short delta-only follow-up rather than another completion-style summary.\n\n## 5. Report completion on Linear\n\nWhen the implementation and local verification are complete, create or update one canonical comment on the original Linear issue containing:\n\n- What was implemented.\n- Important behavior or design decisions.\n- Files or system areas changed.\n- Tests and checks run, with pass/fail counts where available.\n- Any verification that could not be run and the exact reason.\n- Remaining risks, follow-ups, migrations, or rollout requirements.\n- A clear statement that the work is local and awaiting the master's approval for branch and pull-request publication.\n\nIf this agent already posted a canonical completion comment for the current work cycle, update that comment rather than creating another. Retain the returned comment ID for the publication update.\n\nDo not post a completion comment if required work is still incomplete. Post a blocker update instead when progress cannot continue.\n\nAfter commenting, summarize the same state to the master and explicitly request publication approval.\n\n## 6. Approval gate\n\nWait for an explicit approval from the master to publish.\n\nApproval must clearly authorize creating the issue branch and pull request for this completed work. Silence, issue assignment, a Linear status change, an earlier request to implement, or approval from another agent does not satisfy this gate.\n\nIf the master requests changes instead:\n\n1. Apply and verify them.\n2. Add a delta-only follow-up comment when the changes are material, covering only the new fixes, decisions, and verification. Otherwise update the canonical comment.\n3. Request approval again.\n\n## 7. Create the issue branch and pull request\n\nOnly after explicit approval:\n\n1. Re-read the Linear issue and its newest comments for late changes.\n2. Recheck repository status and confirm the repository default branch.\n3. Confirm the local change set contains only work for this issue.\n4. Create a new branch whose name is exactly the Linear issue identifier, for example `SKI-123`. Do not add a prefix or description.\n5. If that exact branch already exists locally or remotely, inspect it and ask the master before reusing, overwriting, renaming, or force-pushing it.\n6. Stage only issue-scoped files.\n7. Create an intentional commit referencing the Linear issue identifier.\n8. Push the new branch without force.\n9. Create a pull request into the repository's confirmed default branch.\n10. Include the Linear issue link, implementation summary, verification results, rollout notes, and known limitations in the pull-request body.\n11. Never merge the pull request unless the master separately requests it.\n\nAfter the pull request is created, update the canonical completion comment with a Publication subsection containing the branch name, commit, pull-request link, target branch, any verification performed since the previous update, and remaining review or rollout requirements.\n\nDo not create a second completion-style comment merely because the work moved from local completion to a branch or pull request. If the canonical comment cannot be updated, post a short publication-only follow-up containing only the new publication information.\n\n## Failure rules\n\n- Never invent issue details, repository identity, branch names, test results, comments, approvals, or pull-request URLs.\n- Never mark the Linear issue done or change its status unless the master explicitly requests that state change.\n- Never use destructive Git operations or force push.\n- If Linear or repository tools are unavailable, report the missing capability and stop at the affected step.\n- Before writing a comment, skip or update any existing comment that already communicates the same state for the same work revision.\n- If publication partially succeeds, report exact external state and resume from that state; do not duplicate comments, branches, commits, or pull requests.\n",
