      "title": "Make McpFn Cloudflare Worker bundles resilient to mixed Zod entry points",
      "url": "https://linear.app/21n/issue/MCP-4/make-mcpfn-cloudflare-worker-bundles-resilient-to-mixed-zod-entry",
      "description": "## Executive summary\n\nMcpFn currently exposes a Cloudflare Workers startup failure when a consumer bundles McpFn with root `zod` imports. The observed Skillplane deployment fails before the Worker starts with `TypeError: Class2 is not a constructor`. The durable fix belongs at the McpFn runtime and packaging boundary, with an upstream MCP SDK correction where practical.\n\n## Problem\n\n`@mcpfn/core@0.0.4` depends on `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk@1.29.0`. The SDK loads runtime schemas from `zod/v4`, while representative consumers such as Skillplane import `zod` from the package root. Wrangler/esbuild can emit an initialization order in which the SDK evaluates `z.custom(...)` before the underlying Zod constructor is initialized. A dry-run bundle succeeds because it does not execute the Worker, while workerd startup reproduces the exception.\n\nAn experimental consumer-side alias that normalizes `zod/v4` to the root ESM entry removes the failure, establishing the mixed-entry-point bundle as the cause. That alias is evidence, not the desired permanent consumer contract.\n\n## Expected outcome\n\nPublished McpFn packages can be consumed by Cloudflare Workers applications that also use root `zod` imports without requiring application-local aliases or shims. McpFn release validation detects bundle-time initialization regressions by starting a representative Worker, not only producing a bundle.\n\n## Goals\n\n* Remove or contain the runtime dependency path that causes mixed Zod entry-point initialization failures.\n* Prefer an upstream MCP SDK fix or compatible SDK upgrade when it provides a stable solution.\n* Add a representative Wrangler/workerd startup regression test to McpFn release gates.\n* Publish patched McpFn packages with migration or compatibility notes.\n* Preserve existing MCP protocol and authorization behavior.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n* Skillplane dependency rollout or topology deployment.\n* Skillplane-specific Wrangler configuration cleanup.\n* Changes to authentication semantics, tenant policy, or tool behavior.\n* Production deployment as part of backlog capture.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n* A representative Cloudflare Worker importing published McpFn and root `zod` bundles and reaches ready state under workerd.\n* No consumer-local `zod/v4` alias or compatibility shim is required.\n* The regression test executes the built Worker and would fail on the current `Class2 is not a constructor` behavior.\n* Existing McpFn protocol/runtime and authorization tests remain green.\n* Updated McpFn packages are published and their compatibility requirements are documented.\n* The chosen fix accounts for the upstream MCP SDK packaging behavior rather than relying only on a Wrangler version bump.\n\n## Dependencies and risks\n\n* Upstream dependency: `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` and its `zod/v4` runtime imports.\n* Bundler and workerd behavior may differ across Wrangler versions; test the supported range.\n* Bundling or externalization changes can alter package size, tree-shaking, or runtime resolution.\n* A temporary McpFn mitigation may need removal after an upstream SDK release.\n\n## Open questions\n\n* Can McpFn eliminate the top-level SDK schema import that triggers the ordering problem without weakening runtime validation?\n* Should McpFn carry a packaging mitigation until the MCP SDK publishes a corrected entry-point strategy?\n* Which Wrangler/workerd versions define the supported release matrix?\n\n## Source context\n\nSupersedes the upstream portion of SKI-18: [SKI-18](https://linear.app/21n/issue/SKI-18/fix-cloudflare-dev-mcp-worker-upload-failure)",
